\chapter{Relational MIR facts and snapshot analysis}
\label{ch:86}
\index{Relational MIR facts and snapshot analysis}

The optional Novostone integration projects verified MIR to a versioned relational fact schema. Embedded Datalog derives queries such as reachability, call/effect closure and edge-aware liveness. CCDB snapshots and exact identity govern publication and reuse across fact deltas. Facts describe a checked program. Transformations still need the verifier and the relevant preservation checks.

\section{Follow a changed edge}
Start with a branch with two successors, and find its edge and live-value facts. Simplify the branch or replace the MIR snapshot in the supported API. Compare the changed facts and derived query results. Pay attention to block arguments: their value is passed on a specific edge, so a use on that edge need not imply a use everywhere in the source block. Bound the work and report whether the analysis completed. Different facts may use similar names to the operation-level MIR, but the latter is the execution authority.

\section{Compute a call closure across revisions}
Use the supplied analysis example: \OnxCodeTerm{even} calls
\OnxCodeTerm{odd}, which in turn calls \OnxCodeTerm{even}. Draw one
direct arrow for each callsite. Following one arrow establishes the two
cross-function pairs in the derived \OnxCodeTerm{Calls} relation. Following
two arrows establishes a pair from each function to itself. These self
pairs come from nonempty paths around the cycle. A leaf with no calls
does not acquire a self pair just because it is listed as a function.
In the same way, an I/O effect in a callee can appear in a caller's
conservative effect summary without there being a print instruction at
the callsite.

Take a query on this first snapshot. Replace the body of \OnxCodeTerm{odd}
with a type-correct leaf returning a Boolean constant. After verification
and publication, the direct arrow back to \OnxCodeTerm{even} disappears,
so neither former self pair follows. The original query still owns a
usable view of the old program; attempting to consume it as a result
for the new snapshot must fail the current-identity check. Rerunning
the query on the new snapshot answers the new question.

As a control, change only a source location. The mapping records location
facts, so this too can be a new program identity. The call relation itself
has not changed and its cached computation may be reused. Equal answer
rows therefore do not establish interchangeable snapshot identities.
Look at the canonical MIR and rule identity in a report before explaining
an apparent cache hit as reuse of executable code.

